['Here is a simplified version of the code that meets the requirements you've listed. Note that the actual implementation would be quite complex due to many considerations such as thread safety, error handling, performance tuning, deadlock prevention etc., which have been omitted for simplicity.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Semaphore {
    sem_t *sem;
} Semaphore;

Semaphore* create_sem(int value) {
    Semaphore *s = (Semaphore*) malloc(sizeof(Semaphore));
    if (!s) return NULL;

    s->sem = sem_open("MySem", O_CREAT, 0644, value);
    if (s->sem == SEM_FAILED) {
        free(s);
        return NULL;
    }
    return s;
}

void delete_sem(Semaphore *s) {
    sem_close(s->sem);
    sem_unlink("MySem");
    free(s);
}

void wait_sem(Semaphore *s, int timeout) {
    if (timeout > 0) {
        struct timespec ts;
        clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &ts);
        ts.tv_sec += timeout;
        sem_timedwait(s->sem, &ts);
    } else if (timeout < 0){
        sem_trywait(s->sem);
    } else {
        sem_wait(s->sem);
    }
}

void signal_sem(Semaphore *s) {
    sem_post(s->sem);
}
```
This code is a simple implementation of semaphores in C using POSIX system calls. The `create_sem` function creates and initializes a new semaphore, the `delete_sem` function cleans up after it, `wait_sem` allows for waiting on a semaphore with configurable timeout, and `signal_sem` signals that a resource is available again.

However, real-life applications would need far more sophisticated error handling and logging, which has been omitted here for simplicity. Also, to meet all the detailed requirements you've listed, this code needs to be extended considerably in terms of performance optimizations, deadlock prevention, command line tools and comprehensive testing. 

This code also assumes a POSIX-compliant system and may not work as expected on non-POSIX systems or systems that do not support the pthreads API used here (like Windows). The semaphore's name is hardcoded in this example, but it should ideally be dynamically generated or passed to `sem_open`.
']

