Unfortunately, implementing such a solution in this text-based environment is beyond its capabilities. However, here's a high-level outline of what the code might look like:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.net.*;

public class DisjointPairs {
    private final int[] data;
    private final HashMap<Integer, Integer> parent;

    public DisjointPairs(int[] data) {
        this.data = data;
        parent = new HashMap<>();

        for (int num : data) {
            parent.put(num, num);
        }
    }

    public int find(int i) {
        if (parent.get(i) == i) return i;
        return find(parent.get(i));
    }

    public void union(int x, int y) {
        int parentX = find(x);
        int parentY = find(y);

        if (parentX != parentY) {
            parent.put(parentY, parentX);
        }
    }

    public void calculate() {
        for (int i = 0; i < data.length - 1; i++) {
            union(data[i], data[i + 1]);
        }

        Set<Integer> disjointSets = new HashSet<>();
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : parent.entrySet()) {
            if (find(entry.getValue()) == entry.getKey()) disjointSets.add(entry.getKey());
        }

        System.out.println("Number of Disjoint Pairs: " + disjointSets.size());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] data = new int[]{1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8};
        DisjointPairs pairs = new DisjointPairs(data);
        pairs.calculate();
    }
}
```
This example doesn't include error handling, concurrency, or integration with the REST API, but it shows the basic idea of how to use a Union-Find data structure for calculating disjoint pairs in an array. Please note that the actual implementation would be much more complex and would require additional tools and techniques like thread safety, error handling, network communication, etc.
