["Your task is complex and involves a wide range of different components such as distributed systems, concurrent programming, caching strategies, error handling, logging & monitoring, etc. Here's a basic outline for each of your requirements. 

1. Key-value storage with customizable TTL policies
```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <chrono>
class Cache {
public:
    void put(const std::string& key, const std::string& value, std::chrono::seconds ttl) {
        data[key] = value;
        expiryTime[key] = std::chrono::system_clock::now() + ttl;
    }
    bool get(const std::string& key, std::string& value) {
        auto it = data.find(key);
        if (it == data.end()) return false;

        // Check expiry
        if (expiryTime[key] < std::chrono::system_clock::now()) {
            data.erase(it);
            expiryTime.erase(key);
            return false;
        }
        value = it->second;
        return true;
    }
private:
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> data;
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::chrono::system_clock::time_point> expiryTime;
};
```
2. Concurrent read and write operations across multiple client nodes
This would require a distributed system, which is complex to implement in C++ without external libraries or tools like ZeroMQ, gRPC etc. You might want to consider using existing distributed systems databases such as Apache Cassandra or Redis Cluster. 

3. Background workers for monitoring and evicting expired entries
This would involve creating threads that periodically scan the cache for expired items, but be careful with thread safety. Here's a very simplified example:
```cpp
#include <thread>
void Cache::startWorker() {
    workerThread = std::thread([this](){
        while(true) {
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1)); // sleep for 1 second

            auto now = std::chrono::system_clock::now();
            for (auto it = expiryTime.begin(); it != expiryTime.end() /* not safe to increment */; ) {
                if (it->second < now) {
                    data.erase(it->first);  // this is still not safe without proper locking mechanisms
                    it = expiryTime.erase(it);
                } else {
                    ++it;
                }
            }
        }
    });
}
```
