Next, we could use Spring's `@Autowired` and `@Qualifier` annotations to inject the correct gateway based on some configuration or runtime decision:

```java
@RestController
public class PaymentController {

    private Map<String, PaymentGateway> paymentGateways;
    private FraudDetectionService fraudDetectionService;

    @Autowired
    public PaymentController(@Qualifier("stripe") PaymentGateway stripeGateway, 
                             @Qualifier("paypal") PaymentGateway payPalGateway,
                             FraudDetectionService fraudDetectionService) {
        this.paymentGateways = new HashMap<>();
        this.paymentGateways.put("stripe", stripeGateway);
        this.paymentGateways.put("paypal", payPalGateway);
        this.fraudDetectionService = fraudDetectionService;
    }

    @PostMapping("/payments")
    public ResponseEntity<String> processPayment(@RequestBody Payment payment) {
        if (fraudDetectionService.isFraudulent(payment)) {
            return new ResponseEntity<>("Fraud detected", HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
        }
        PaymentGateway gateway = paymentGateways.get(payment.getPaymentMethod());
        if (gateway == null) {
            return new ResponseEntity<>("Unsupported payment method", HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
        }
        try {
            gateway.processPayment(payment);
            return new ResponseEntity<>("Payment processed successfully", HttpStatus.OK);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return new ResponseEntity<>("Error processing payment: " + e.getMessage(), HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
        }
    }
}
``` 
This is a very simplified example and real-world applications would require much more complex solutions, including integration with message brokers for failed transactions and distributed tracing tools for monitoring performance. The fraud detection service can be implemented using machine learning techniques or rules engines, depending on your use case. 

