Here's a very simplified and abstracted version of how one might create a basic graph data structure in C++. This code doesn't meet all the requirements outlined, but it serves as a starting point:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>

class Graph {
public:
    using Node = int;
    using Edge = std::pair<Node, Node>;

    void addNode(const Node &node) {
        if (adjacencyList.find(node) == adjacencyList.end()) {
            adjacencyList[node] = {};
        }
    }

    void removeNode(const Node &node) {
        adjacencyList.erase(node);
        for (auto& pair : adjacencyList) {
            auto& edges = pair.second;
            edges.erase(std::remove_if(edges.begin(), edges.end(), [&](const Edge &e){ return e.first == node || e.second == node; }), edges.end());
        }
    }

    void addEdge(const Node &from, const Node &to) {
        if (adjacencyList.find(from) != adjacencyList.end() && adjacencyList.find(to) != adjacencyList.end()) {
            adjacencyList[from].push_back({ from, to });
        } else {
            std::cerr << "Can't add edge for non-existing node" << std::endl;
        }
    }

private:
    std::unordered_map<Node, std::vector<Edge>> adjacencyList;
};
```
Please note that the above example is a very simple and basic implementation of a graph data structure in C++. It doesn't handle error conditions or concurrency issues which are essential for handling large scale, distributed systems. Developing such a system would be quite complex and require significant expertise in designing efficient and reliable distributed systems.
