```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.SerializationUtils;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class DeserializerService {
    private static final int MAX_THREADS = 100; // maximum concurrent threads
    private static final long INITIAL_BACKOFF_MS = 100;
    private static final double BACKOFF_MULTIPLIER = 2.0;
    private ExecutorService executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(MAX_THREADS, MAX_THREADS, 0L, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS, new LinkedBlockingQueue<Runnable>());

    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(DeserializerService.class);

    public <T> T deserializeObject(byte[] data) throws ExecutionException, InterruptedException {
        Future<T> future = executor.submit(() -> (T) SerializationUtils.deserialize(new ByteArrayInputStream(data)));
        return future.get();
    }

    public <T> T deserializeWithRetry(byte[] data, Class<T> clazz) {
        long backoffMs = INITIAL_BACKOFF_MS;

        for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) { // retry 5 times before giving up
            try {
                return deserializeObject(data);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.error("Deserialization failed for class " + clazz.getName() + ". Retrying...", e);

                if (i < 4) // don't sleep after the last retry
                    Thread.sleep(backoffMs);

                backoffMs *= BACKOFF_MULTIPLIER;
            }
        }

        logger.error("Deserialization failed for class " + clazz.getName() + " after 5 attempts.");
        return null; // or throw exception, depending on your needs
    }
}
```
This code uses a simple ExecutorService to limit the number of concurrent threads. It also includes an exponential backoff mechanism with a maximum of 5 retries for transient deserialization failures. Note that this is just a simplified example and does not include all the features you requested, like integration with logging systems or distributed tracing frameworks. Also please note that SerializationUtils used here is from Apache Commons Lang library which is used to serialize objects. For custom serialization protocols you would have to implement your own logic for deserialization of bytes. 

For more complex scenarios involving multiple nodes, network partitions and ensuring consistency, one might need to look at distributed data processing frameworks like Apache Spark or Apache Flink which provides fault tolerance mechanisms and can handle failures gracefully. For logging and tracing you would have to integrate with appropriate systems. Also note that the code does not follow semantic versioning guidelines as it's simplified for brevity, but in real-world scenario those should be properly handled.
