['The provided task is quite extensive and can't be fully accomplished in this text-based platform due to space limitations. However, I will provide a basic skeleton of the classes required for handling Java serialization, custom deserialization, error handling and concurrent processing.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class Deserializer {
    private ExecutorService executor;

    public Deserializer(int threads) {
        this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(threads);
    }

    public <T> Future<T> deserializeAsync(byte[] bytes, Class<T> clazz) {
        return executor.submit(() -> deserialize(bytes, clazz));
    }

    private <T> T deserialize(byte[] bytes, Class<T> clazz) {
        try (ByteArrayInputStream b = new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes);
             ObjectInput o = new ObjectInputStream(b)) {
            return clazz.cast(o.readObject());
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException | IOException e) {
            throw new DeserializationException("Error deserializing object", e);
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
``` 
Above, we have a simple `Deserializer` class which uses Java's built-in serialization for handling `ObjectInputStream` and `readObject` method to deserialize the bytes into an object of type T. We also create an asynchronous version of the deserialization method using Java's ExecutorService and Future objects for concurrent processing. 

Error handling has been partially implemented in this class with a custom exception `DeserializationException` which is thrown when there are issues during deserialization.

For third-party libraries, you can use Maven or Gradle to manage them as dependencies. For monitoring and profiling tools, you might want to look into Prometheus for metrics collection and Grafana for visualizations, and VisualVM for detailed performance analysis of Java applications.

