['Below is an example code snippet in Java, but designing and implementing a robust distributed caching layer requires advanced knowledge in multiple aspects including Java, microservices architecture, networking, concurrency, databases, messaging queues etc. The given problem statement involves extensive work, spanning various areas of software development which can't be achieved with a single code snippet.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class DistributedCache {
    private Map<String, String> cache; // This will hold the key-value pairs in memory for simplicity 

    public DistributedCache() {
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
    }

    synchronized void put(String key, String value) {
        if (key != null && !key.isEmpty()) {
            cache.put(key, value);
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Key cannot be null or empty");
        }
    }

    synchronized void putAll(Map<String, String> map) {
        for (Map.Entry<String, String> entry : map.entrySet()) {
            put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
        }
    }

    synchronized String get(String key) {
        return cache.getOrDefault(key, null);
    }

    synchronized void remove(String key) {
        cache.remove(key);
    }
}
```
This is a simple in-memory key-value pair store with synchronization for thread safety. To make this a distributed system we need to distribute the data across multiple nodes and ensure consistency across them which involves many complexities including replication, consensus algorithms (Raft/Paxos), network partitions, handling node failures etc.

For external dependencies like Docker registry, databases, message queues, you would need to handle these separately with appropriate SDKs or APIs provided by the services and manage their connections accordingly. 

Dynamic service configurations can be done in a microservices architecture using configuration servers (Spring Cloud Config Server for example) which stores all the configurations of your applications centrally.

