    while(std::getline(input_file, line)) {
        if (divisible_by > 1 && stoi(line) % divisible_by != 0) continue;
        auto val = stoi(line); // You might need to handle exceptions here.
        numbers.push_back(val);
    }

    std::vector<std::future<bool>> futures;

    for (auto& num : numbers) {
        futures.emplace_back(std::async(validate, num));
    }

    int valid = 0, invalid = 0;

    for (auto& future : futures) {
        if (future.get()) ++valid;
        else ++invalid;
    }

    BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(info) << "Valid: " + std::to_string(valid) + " Invalid: " + std::to_string(invalid);
    BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(info) << "Median: " + std::to_string(calculate_median(numbers));

    auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(info) << "Processing took " + std::to_string(std::chrono::duration<double>(end-start).count()) + " seconds";
}

int main() {
    process_csv_file("data.csv", 3); // Replace this with your actual file and divisibility criteria.
    return 0;
}
```
This is a simplified example which doesn't cover all the edge cases you mentioned, but it gives you an idea of how to approach solving this problem in C++. For large files or concurrency, you might want to read the file line by line and process each line asynchronously, validating each number with a different API request running on separate threads. Edge case handling, like network timeouts or invalid API responses, will require specific handling based on how your application interacts with the external API. 
For unit tests, you could use testing frameworks such as Google Test and Mock for C++. You would write tests to cover normal cases (valid numbers, invalid numbers), edge cases (large files, network timeouts, invalid API responses) and error handling.
