['Implementing such a service in Java involves various modules like configuration fetching from remote server, cache management, error handling, thread safety, metrics collection etc. 
Here is a simplified implementation using Spring Boot, Spring Scheduling for periodical updates, Spring Retry for retries, EhCache as in-memory cache and JSON files to persist data. This is just a high level overview and doesn't include all the details such as error handling, metrics collection, thread safety etc.

```java
@Service
public class ConfigurationService {

    @Autowired
    private RestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Autowired
    private CacheManager cacheManager;

    private final String CONFIGURATION_CACHE = "configurationCache";

    //... other methods 

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 5000) // run every 5 seconds
    public void fetchConfigurationUpdates() {
        try {
            ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.getForEntity("http://remote-config-server/configuration", String.class);
            if (response.getStatusCode().is2xxSuccessful()) {
                String configurationJson = response.getBody();
                cacheManager.getCache(CONFIGURATION_CACHE).put("latestConfiguration", configurationJson);

                // Also persist the configuration to JSON file 
                try (PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter("configuration.json")))) {
                    out.println(configurationJson);
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    // handle exception
                }
            } else {
                // Handle error scenario
            }
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            // Retry mechanism using Spring's @Retryable and @Recover annotations 
        }
    }
}
```
The above service fetches configuration updates from a remote server every five seconds. It uses EhCache as the cache manager to store the latest configurations in memory for fast access. The configurations are also stored in JSON files (simulating disk storage) for persistence and recovery purposes.

