['Creating this system would require a lot of code which cannot be fully represented here due to its complexity and length. However, I will provide an example of how you might start implementing a simple real-time data processing pipeline with Kafka and Java. 

Firstly, let's start by setting up a basic producer:

```java
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;

import java.util.Properties;

public class SimpleProducer {
    public static void main(String[] args){
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        props.put("key.serializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put("value.serializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");

        KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props);
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<String, String>("my-topic", Integer.toString(i), Integer.toString(i)));
        }

        producer.close();
    }
}
```

Next, let's create a basic consumer:

```java
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Properties;

public class SimpleConsumer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "test");
        props.put("enable.auto.commit", "true");
        props.put("key.deserializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put("value.deserializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props);
        consumer.subscribe(Arrays.asList("my-topic"));

        while (true) {
            ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(100);
            for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
                System.out.printf("offset = %d, key = %s, value = %s%n", record.offset(), record.key(), record.value());
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a very simplified example and does not include all the features you requested. You would need to add error handling, logging, configuration management, serialization/deserialization of complex data types, integration with IoT devices, social media APIs, financial transaction systems, satellite imagery platforms, caching strategy, routing mechanism, anomaly detection, encryption, OAuth2, machine learning models, monitoring and alerting, disaster recovery plan, etc. Each of these features would require a lot more code and would vary significantly based on the specific requirements and constraints of your use case. Also, please note that Kafka, RabbitMQ, IoT devices, social media APIs, financial transaction systems, satellite imagery platforms are just examples. You might be using different tools/services for these features depending on your specific scenario.
