["Here is a rudimentary implementation of such an advanced module using modern JavaScript features, Promise for asynchronous execution and async/await to manage dependencies between files. This doesn't include everything you mentioned like circular dependency detection or real-time feedback but it gives you a start point to build on top of this:

```javascript
// DependencyManager.js
class DependencyManager {
    constructor() {
        this.loadedFiles = {};
        this.loadingFiles = {};
    }

    async loadFile(filePath) {
        if (this.isLoaded(filePath)) return;

        if (!this.isLoading(filePath)) {
            this.setIsLoading(filePath);

            const fileModule = await import(filePath);
            this.markAsLoaded(filePath, fileModule);
        } else {
            // Wait for the other thread to finish loading this file first.
            while (!this.isLoaded(filePath)) {
                await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10));
            }
        }

        return this.loadedFiles[filePath];
    }

    isLoading(filePath) {
        return Boolean(this.loadingFiles[filePath]);
    }

    setIsLoading(filePath) {
        this.loadingFiles[filePath] = true;
    }

    markAsLoaded(filePath, fileModule) {
        delete this.loadingFiles[filePath];
        this.loadedFiles[filePath] = fileModule;
    }

    isLoaded(filePath) {
        return Boolean(this.loadedFiles[filePath]);
    }
}
```
This code will only load a file once, and will wait if another thread is loading the same file to ensure thread safety and avoid race conditions. It uses import() function that returns a Promise for dynamic module importing in JavaScript which is built-in support in modern browsers for ES6 modules. 

It doesn't include error handling, circular dependency detection, real-time feedback or integration with an existing web application but these are possible extensions you can make based on this code.
"]

