["Designing an entire health check utility from scratch would be a massive task and go beyond the capabilities of this model. It involves multiple concepts like thread safety, network programming, error handling, memory management, system dependencies etc. Here's how you could start with the circuit breaker and retry mechanism concept for external service status monitoring:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>

class ExternalService {
public:
    bool isUp() {
        // Implement logic to check if the service is up or not
        // This can be a REST API call, DB query etc.
    }
};

class CircuitBreaker {
private:
    int failureThreshold;
    int retryTimePeriod;
    std::chrono::system_clock::time_point lastFailureTime;
public:
    bool allowRequest() {
        if(failureCount < failureThreshold) return true;

        if((std::chrono::system_clock::now() - lastFailureTime).count() > retryTimePeriod) {
            failureCount = std::max(0, --failureCount);
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    void recordFailure() {
        ++failureCount;
        lastFailureTime = std::chrono::system_clock::now();
    }
};

class HealthChecker {
private:
    ExternalService service;
    CircuitBreaker breaker;
public:
    bool isHealthy() {
        if(!breaker.allowRequest()) return false;

        bool result = service.isUp();

        // Record the result in breaker and retry after a delay if required
        if(result) {
            breaker.recordSuccess();
        } else {
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
            return isHealthy();
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```
This code is a simplified illustration and does not cover all the aspects mentioned in your requirements. Each of these classes needs to be designed with more detail as per the actual requirement. For example, you may want to include some kind of configuration service to fetch timeout values etc. 

Additionally, implementing the producer-consumer pattern, multi-tenancy support, thread safety, memory and CPU usage optimization, efficient deployment strategies, adaptive strategies for handling fluctuating workloads etc. would be complex tasks that would involve a lot more code, possibly in separate classes/files to keep things organized.
