['This is an extensive programming task that requires a team of experienced developers to design, implement, and optimize a C library from scratch. Writing code for such a complex system would be beyond the scope of this assistant, as it involves numerous components including dynamic configuration management, secure data retrieval strategies, efficient load balancing, resource cleanup and monitoring, comprehensive logging framework, and user interface design. 

However, I can provide a very simple example to demonstrate how you might manage a pool of network interfaces with C using pthreads for thread safety:

```C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    int id;
    char ip[16];
} NetworkInterface;

NetworkInterface *network_interfaces = NULL;
int num_interfaces = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

void add_interface(const char *ip) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    // Reallocate memory for the new interface
    network_interfaces = realloc(network_interfaces, (num_interfaces + 1) * sizeof(NetworkInterface));
    if (!network_interfaces) {
        perror("Failed to allocate memory");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Add the new interface
    network_interfaces[num_interfaces].id = num_interfaces + 1;
    snprintf(network_interfaces[num_interfaces].ip, sizeof(network_interfaces[num_interfaces].ip), "%s", ip);

    // Update the number of interfaces
    num_interfaces++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void print_interfaces() {
    for (int i = 0; i < num_interfaces; ++i) {
        printf("Interface ID: %d, IP: %s\n", network_interfaces[i].id, network_interfaces[i].ip);
    }
}
```

This is a very basic example and does not include all the features you mentioned such as dynamic configuration management, secure data retrieval strategies, efficient load balancing, etc. However, it should give you an idea of how to start implementing this kind of system with C. 

