Firstly, define your structure that you will use in linked list:

```C
typedef struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    uint32_t RNTI;
    void* RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG; // this can be anything depending on what you are working with
    void* CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG; 
    bool CRCFLAG; // assuming boolean for simplicity, it can also be an enum or int if needed
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;
```

Then create a linked list node that will hold your structure:

```C
typedef struct Node {
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;
```

To generate unique `RNTI` values within a predefined limit and to handle exhaustion, you can use an algorithm like the one below:

```C
bool assignUniqueRnti(Node** head_ref) {
    uint32_t limit = 100; // predefined limit. Replace with your actual value
    static uint32_t count = 0;

    if (count >= limit) {
        printf("RNTI exhaustion: Fallback mechanism initiated\n");
        return false; // or raise an exception, based on how you want to handle exhaustion
    }

    Node* new_node = malloc(sizeof(Node)); 
    new_node->data.RNTI = count++;
    new_node->next = *head_ref;
    *head_ref = new_node;

    return true; // or return the newly assigned RNTI, based on your needs
}
```

For real-time data synchronization with an external API, you would need to implement a retry mechanism around your API calls:

```C
#include <unistd.h> // for usleep function

bool apiCallWithRetry(void* (*apiFunction)(void), int numAttempts) {
    for (int attempt = 0; attempt < numAttempts; ++attempt) {
        void* result = apiFunction();

        if (result != NULL) { // replace with your own logic to validate the result
            return true; 
        } else {
            printf("API call failed. Retrying...\n");
            usleep((1 << attempt) * 1000); // exponential backoff before next retry
        }
    }

    return false;
}
```
Please note that the above is a very basic implementation and might not cover all aspects of your requirements as it's quite complex to implement these in a single function without a proper structure. You need to divide this task into smaller modules and build upon them. For example, you could create separate modules for RNTI generation, real-time data synchronization with external API, error handling etc., which can be easily tested independently. Then combine all these modules to form your final solution.
