['This task is very broad, complex, and potentially a project in its own right. Here's a simple example using Git (with some assumptions) for the first requirement. Note that this is by no means a complete solution but rather a starting point for understanding how you might approach such a problem with C++. The complexity of your actual implementation would greatly depend on many factors including the specifics of your version control system, concurrency level, and data structure requirements.

```cpp
#include <git2.h> // You need libgit2 to work with git

class GitManager {
private:
    std::string repoPath;
    git_repository *repo = nullptr;

public:
    GitManager(const std::string &path) : repoPath(path) {}

    ~GitManager() {
        if (repo != nullptr)
            git_repository_free(repo);
    }

    bool initializeRepo() {
        int error = git_repository_open(&repo, repoPath.c_str());
        return error == 0;
    }

    // Assumes that 'commitMessage' is a string and 'files' is a vector of file paths to be committed.
    bool commitChanges(const std::string &commitMessage, const std::vector<std::string> &files) {
        git_signature *sig;

        if (git_signature_now(&sig, "Your Name", "your@email.com") < 0) 
            return false;

        git_index *index = nullptr;
        git_repository_index(&index, repo);

        for(auto &file : files){
            git_index_add_bypath(index, file.c_str());
        }

        git_oid treeId;
        if (git_index_write_tree(&treeId, index) < 0) 
            return false;

        git_tree *tree = nullptr;
        if (git_tree_lookup(&tree, repo, &treeId) < 0) 
            return false;

        git_oid commitId;
        git_commit_create_v(
          &commitId,             /* Store the OID of the new commit here */
          repo,                   /* Repository where to create the commit */
          "HEAD",                 /* Name of the parent commit. For initial commit, this is 'nullptr' */
          sig,                    /* The author of the commit */
          sig,                    /* The commiter of the commit */
          0,                     /* Encoding for message, typically UTF-8 */
          commitMessage.c_str(),  /* Commit message */
          tree,                   /* Tree to create the commit from */
          1,                      /* Number of parents in 'parent' array */
          nullptr                 /* Array of parent commits (or NULL) */
        );

        git_index_write(index);

        return true;
    }

    // More methods for retrieving and manipulating nodes/edges would go here...
};
```
This class provides an example of a basic Git repository manager. You can initialize the repository, commit changes to it, retrieve data from it, etc. Each method is meant to perform a specific operation (e.g., `commitChanges`), but you'd need to add many more methods as well for full functionality.
']

["This is an extensive project that involves complex programming concepts. While it's not feasible to generate the entire code base here due to its size and complexity, I can provide you with a simplified high-level overview of how to approach this task using C++17 features. 

We start by defining our `NetworkManager` class which will handle network connections and encryption:

```cpp
class NetworkManager {
private:
    std::vector<std::future<void>> asyncTasks;
    std::atomic<bool> isShutdown{false};

public:
    void addTask(std::function<void()> task) {
        asyncTasks.push_back(std::async(task));
    }

    void shutdown() {
        isShutdown = true;
        for (auto& task : asyncTasks) {
            if (task.valid()) {
                try {
                    // Wait on the task to finish
                    task.get();
                } catch(...) {}
            }
        }
    }
};
```

In this `NetworkManager` class, we are using `std::future` and `std::async` for asynchronous processing of tasks. We're also using a shutdown flag to signal that all tasks should be completed before the manager is destroyed. 

