- Note that we are using `this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(2))` to simulate work, which is a common practice in multithreaded applications. You would typically replace this with your actual code doing some useful work. 

Remember that C++11 introduces the `std::async` function for easy and more robust parallel execution of tasks. It returns an object representing the asynchronous computation. Using this mechanism, you can easily manage memory without worrying about manual thread joining or detaching. 

